Subject: Skip the empty register liveness bitmap walk

What this does
  Skips the block liveness extension when a function has no register values.
  This avoids forming pointers from absent bitmaps.

Why we need it
  The register allocator does not allocate liveness bitmaps when a function has
  no SSA values. The later interval extension still formed pointers from those
  null bitmaps, which UBSan reports as undefined behavior.

What it adds
  An empty-value guard on the block walk.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -59376,7 +59376,10 @@
          * body would be free to take the register. So every value's interval is
          * grown to cover each block it is live in, start as well as end.
          */
-        for (b = 0; b < n_blocks; b++) {
+        /* The bitsets are absent when no register values exist. In that case
+           there is no liveness to extend, and even adding a zero offset to the
+           null pointers is undefined behavior in C. */
+        for (b = 0; words > 0 && b < n_blocks; b++) {
             uint64_t *bin, *bout;
             if (in_depth[b] < 0)
                 continue;
